# Dashboard JavaScript Rules

For dashboard JS files:

```txt
public/js/financial.js
public/js/growth.js
public/js/operations.js
public/js/intelligence.js
public/js/marketplace-v2.js
public/js/organizer-crm.js
```

Every fetch must:

* check `response.ok`
* verify JSON response
* catch errors
* avoid unhandled promise rejections
* remove skeleton loaders on failure
* render friendly error cards
* log technical errors only to console

Use a safe fetch helper pattern:

```js
async function fetchLNKupJson(url, options = {}) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(url, {
      credentials: 'same-origin',
      ...options,
      headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        'X-WP-Nonce': window.lnkupRest?.nonce || '',
        ...(options.headers || {})
      }
    });

    const contentType = response.headers.get('content-type') || '';

    if (!contentType.includes('application/json')) {
      const text = await response.text();
      console.error('Expected JSON but received:', text);
      throw new Error('Invalid server response.');
    }

    const json = await response.json();

    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(json.message || json.code || 'Request failed.');
    }

    return json;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('LNKup API error:', error);
    return {
      success: false,
      error: error.message || 'Unable to load data.'
    };
  }
}
```

Never leave infinite skeleton loading.

